Installing mpi-sppy
===================

Full instructions, including platform-specific MPI setup, are in the
Quick Start chapter of the documentation:
https://mpi-sppy.readthedocs.io/en/latest/quick_start.html

Requirements
------------
* Python 3.9 or newer
* Pyomo >= 6.4 and numpy (installed automatically)
* A Pyomo-compatible solver. Open-source choices include HiGHS
  (pip install highspy), Cbc, Ipopt, and SCIP; commercial choices are
  CPLEX, Gurobi, and Xpress. No solver is installed automatically.
* For parallel decomposition: an MPI implementation (OpenMPI or MPICH)
  and mpi4py. The extensive form runs without MPI.
* Optional: scipy, pandas, matplotlib, dill, and mip (python-mip, which
  provides MPS/LP/SMPS input via Cbc). Install them all with the
  [extras] option below.

Install
-------
    git clone https://github.com/Pyomo/mpi-sppy.git
    cd mpi-sppy
    pip install -e ".[mpi,extras]"

If mpi4py is already installed (for example from conda), use
pip install -e ".[extras]" instead. A release is also on PyPI
(pip install mpi-sppy), but it lags the GitHub repository.

Verify the installation
-----------------------
1. Check that MPI works:

       mpiexec -n 2 mpi-sppy-one-sided-test

   No error output means MPI is usable.

2. Run the tests. The test suite looks for CPLEX, Gurobi, or Xpress;
   without one of them most tests are skipped. The free community
   editions from pip are enough, and are what continuous integration
   uses:

       pip install -e ".[test]"
       pip install cplex
       python -m pytest mpisppy/tests/test_ef_ph.py
       mpiexec -np 2 python -m mpi4py mpisppy/tests/test_with_cylinders.py

   Each command reports passes and failures.

About the tests
---------------
The tests are unittest/pytest modules in mpisppy/tests, generally one
module per feature (e.g., test_ef_ph.py covers the extensive form and
progressive hedging; test_with_cylinders.py covers the hub-and-spoke
runs under MPI). The full list, and how each is run, is in
.github/workflows/test_pr_and_main.yml; run_coverage.bash runs them all.
To add a test, add a module to mpisppy/tests and register it in both of
those files.
